  "narrative": "I audited the new discovery results using the signed intervals as supplied and the approximate 10% clinically meaningful dependency scale used earlier: about 0.028 for STAT3, 0.024-0.026 for most dependency scores near a mean of -0.25, about 0.030 for WRN, and about 0.041 for SMARCA2. More negative dependency values mean stronger dependency; for a direction of -1, positive signed estimates therefore mean stronger dependency in the exposed group. H27 is the only clinically interesting but borderline new result. ERBB2 amplification among PTEN-intact cells gives a signed STAT3 difference of +0.032 (95% Welch interval +0.003 to +0.060), which exceeds the approximate 0.028 threshold at the point estimate and is directionally consistent with the idea that ERBB2 signaling may increase STAT3 dependence. However, the lower confidence limit is well below the clinically meaningful threshold, the exposed stratum contains only 145 amplified lines, and the result could be influenced by co-amplification or lineage structure. It is therefore not strong enough to accept without confirmation. I requested independent validation for H27. H35 is unambiguous and acceptably robust: MSI-high versus non-MSI-high among TP53-intact cells shows an enormous signed WRN dependency difference of +0.780 (interval +0.747 to +0.813), far above the approximate WRN threshold. This refines the accepted H6 MSI-high/WRN association and does not depend on TP53-loss co-occurrence, but it remains a dataset-level association, not causal or generally generalizable. H28 tests whether the accepted CDKN2A-loss effect on MDM2 dependency is modified by TP53 loss. The signed interaction is near null, +0.008, with a very wide interval (-0.077 to +0.093) and only 81 exposed TP53-loss/CDKN2A-loss cells in the cell-size vector. This is underpowered and cannot adjudicate attenuation; I leave the claim unresolved but closed as a discovery question, and instead registered a simpler follow-up stratum among T